<article class="article">
<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Long-stay Tourists: Break-Even Thinking for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>Long-stay tourists hold one advantage in the rent-versus-buy debate that nobody else in Hanoi has: a horizon printed on a ticket. Residents guess their duration, students borrow semesters, but a visitor knows exactly when the riding ends, and a known horizon turns break-even from a philosophy into a worksheet. This guide builds that worksheet honestly, refusing the seductive universal number that does not exist, holding the rental side to its posted, verified figures, and leaving every ownership pencil line clearly labelled as a pencil line.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk audits visitor break-even sketches with a tutor&rsquo;s patience and a shop&rsquo;s neutrality, because it rents machines and never sells them and therefore has no favourite answer. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">The Number That Does Not Exist</a></li>
